WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.440
Okay, so now let's have a quick look to the specifications of the server layer.

00:03.480 --> 00:09.560
So the main objective to have clean and standardized data we have to prepare the data before going to

00:09.600 --> 00:10.520
the gold layer.

00:10.520 --> 00:13.360
And we will be building tables inside the server layer.

00:13.360 --> 00:17.280
And the way of loading the data from the bronze to the silver is full load.

00:17.280 --> 00:19.840
So that means we're going to truncate and then insert.

00:19.840 --> 00:21.960
And here we're going to have a lot of data transformations.

00:21.960 --> 00:23.200
So we're going to clean the data.

00:23.200 --> 00:26.360
We're going to bring normalization standardizations.

00:26.360 --> 00:28.160
We're going to derive new columns.

00:28.160 --> 00:30.640
We will be doing as well data enrichments.

00:30.640 --> 00:33.280
So a lot of things to be done in the data transformation.

00:33.280 --> 00:36.000
But we will not be building any new data model.

00:36.000 --> 00:37.560
So those are the specifications.

00:37.560 --> 00:40.000
And we have to commit ourselves to this scope okay.

00:40.040 --> 00:45.240
So now building the DDL script for the server layer going to be way easier than the bronze, because

00:45.240 --> 00:50.800
the definition and the structure of each table in the silver can be identical to the bronze layer.

00:50.840 --> 00:52.520
We are not doing anything new.

00:52.520 --> 00:57.800
So all what you have to do is to take the DDL script from the bronze layer and just go and search and

00:57.800 --> 00:59.160
replace for the schema.

00:59.200 --> 01:04.720
I'm just using the Notepad++ for the scripts, so I'm going to go over here and say replace the bronze

01:04.720 --> 01:08.480
dots with silver dots, and I'm going to go and replace all.

01:08.480 --> 01:15.220
So with that now all the DDL is targeting the schema server layer, which is exactly what we need.

01:15.260 --> 01:15.660
All right.

01:15.700 --> 01:21.060
Now, before we execute our new DDL script for the server, we have to talk about something called the

01:21.060 --> 01:22.380
metadata columns.

01:22.420 --> 01:28.660
They are additional columns or fields that the data engineers add to each table that don't come directly

01:28.660 --> 01:34.780
from the source systems, but the data engineers use it in order to provide extra information for each

01:34.780 --> 01:35.420
record.

01:35.460 --> 01:41.820
Like, we can add a column called Create date is when the record was loaded, or an update date when

01:41.820 --> 01:43.460
the record got updated.

01:43.460 --> 01:48.780
Or we can add the source system in order to understand the origin of the data that we have.

01:48.820 --> 01:54.660
Or sometimes we can add the file location in order to understand the lineage from which file the data

01:54.660 --> 01:55.340
came from.

01:55.380 --> 02:00.860
Those are great tool if you have data issue in your data warehouse, if there is like corrupt data and

02:00.860 --> 02:01.460
so on.

02:01.620 --> 02:06.220
This can help you to track exactly where this issue happened and when.

02:06.260 --> 02:10.940
And as well, it is great in order to understand whether I have gap in my data, especially if you are

02:10.940 --> 02:12.300
doing incremental loads.

02:12.300 --> 02:17.780
It is like putting labels on everything and you will thank yourself later when you start using them

02:17.780 --> 02:20.860
in hard times as you have an issue in your data warehouse.

02:20.940 --> 02:22.940
So now back to our DDL scripts and all.

02:22.940 --> 02:24.940
What you have to do is to go and do the following.

02:24.940 --> 02:30.720
So for example, for the first table I will go and add at the end one more extra column.

02:30.720 --> 02:37.080
So it starts with the prefix DW as we have defined in the naming convention, and then underscore let's

02:37.080 --> 02:41.640
have the create date and the data type can be date time to.

02:41.680 --> 02:45.200
And now what we can do is we can go and add a default value for it.

02:45.200 --> 02:48.240
I want the database to generate these informations automatically.

02:48.240 --> 02:51.200
We don't have to specify that in any ETL scripts.

02:51.200 --> 02:53.640
So which value is going to be the get date.

02:53.840 --> 02:59.840
So each record is going to be inserted in this table will get automatically a value from the current

02:59.840 --> 03:00.560
date and time.

03:00.560 --> 03:03.480
So now as you can see the naming convention it is very important.

03:03.480 --> 03:05.720
All those columns comes from the source system.

03:05.720 --> 03:09.560
And only this one column comes from the data engineer of the data warehouse.

03:09.600 --> 03:10.720
Okay, so that's it.

03:10.720 --> 03:13.880
Let's go and repeat the same thing for all other tables.

03:13.880 --> 03:18.600
So I will just go and add this piece of information for each DDL.

03:20.560 --> 03:21.160
All right.

03:21.160 --> 03:22.640
So I think that's it all.

03:22.640 --> 03:27.240
What you have to do is now to go and execute the whole DDL script for the server layer.

03:27.280 --> 03:28.120
Let's go and do that.

03:28.120 --> 03:28.520
All right.

03:28.520 --> 03:29.040
Perfect.

03:29.040 --> 03:29.880
There's no errors.

03:29.920 --> 03:33.160
Let's go and refresh the tables on the Object explorer.

03:33.160 --> 03:36.800
And with that as you can see we have six tables for the server layer.

03:36.800 --> 03:42.000
It is identical to the bronze layer but we have one extra column for the metadata.
